# Should the government nudge us?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Thaler and Sunstein propose? Is it respectful paternalism or manipulation?"
next: {"title":"Are people as irrational as the experiments say?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/90-are-people-as-irrational-as-the.cast"}

## No neutral counter
sources:
    - id: rozin2011
      title: 'Nudge to nobesity I: Minor changes in accessibility decrease food intake'
      authors: Rozin, Scott, Dingley, Urbanek, Jiang, Kaltenbach
      year: 2011
      finding: In a pay-by-weight cafeteria salad bar, moving a food about 10 inches further away or swapping spoon for tongs reduced intake by roughly 8–16%.
    - id: thaler2008
      title: 'Nudge: Improving Decisions about Health, Wealth, and Happiness'
      authors: Richard H. Thaler, Cass R. Sunstein
      year: 2008
      finding: Choice architecture is unavoidable; a nudge alters behaviour predictably without forbidding options or significantly changing economic incentives, and must be easy and cheap to avoid; libertarian paternalism steers people toward choices that make them better off as judged by themselves.

    shape counter #3d3833 shape rect x 500 y 395 width 780 height 46 style.fill #e9dfcf hidden true
    icon apple size 100 set twemoji x 250 y 475 or ["apple"] icon_look picture of "red apple" hidden true
    icon chips size 100 set twemoji x 500 y 475 or ["fries"] icon_look picture of "french fries" hidden true
    icon cake size 100 set twemoji x 750 y 475 or ["cake"] icon_look picture of shortcake hidden true
    arrow push #b5482e from.x 815 from.y 475 to.x 905 to.y 475 hidden true
    text rozin "A few inches further → taken less" #b5482e cites ["rozin2011"] x 640 y 575 font_size 28 hidden true
    text noneutral "No neutral design" #b5482e x 500 y 605 font_size 44 hidden true
    portrait thaler x 100 y 228 width 140 of "Richard Thaler" cameo true hidden true
    text book "Nudge (2008)" #8f887c cites ["thaler2008"] x 500 y 545 font_size 30 hidden true
    text judged "“as judged by themselves”" #2f6b8f cites ["thaler2008"] x 500 y 545 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t1 "✓ Forbids nothing" #3d3833 x 225 y 310 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t2 "✓ Prices unchanged" #3d3833 x 500 y 310 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t3 "✓ Easy to avoid" #3d3833 x 775 y 310 font_size 28 hidden true
    text lp "libertarian paternalism" #2f6b8f x 500 y 225 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards sortq size 0.8 check each hidden true
        Nudge: Fruit at eye level
        Not a nudge: Tax on sugary drinks
        Nudge: Text reminder
        Not a nudge: Ban on smoking in bars
        Nudge: 'Most people pay on time' letter, Calories on the menu
    text fail "Tax: price changes · Ban: option gone" #b5482e x 500 y 228 font_size 32 hidden true
    text canteen "Harbourtown school canteen" #8f887c x 500 y 325 font_size 28 hidden true
    text who "Who steers? Toward what? Visible?" #2f6b8f x 500 y 228 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "No neutral counter"

Should the government nudge us, steering what we choose without forbidding anything? Start in Harbourtow (@draw canteen@)n's school canteen, at the counter.
    draw counter

B: The cook has to put someth (@draw apple@)ing first: f (@draw chips@)ruit, chips (@draw cake@) or cake.

And position matters. In one cafeteria experiment, mov (@draw push@)ing a dish just a few inches further away c (@draw rozin@)ut how much people took.

    quiz "Which order leaves the children's choices untouched?" judge false voice b
        intro: Suppose the cook wants to stay out of it entirely.
        right: None of them. Alphabetical puts the apple first, random steers different children each day, and best-sellers first locks in last year's habits.
        * Alphabetical
        * Random each day
        * Best-sellers first
        * None of them

    erase push rozin

    erase canteen

Every order s (@highlight apple chips cake@)teers someone. Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein call the cook a choice architect, and say there is no neutral d (@draw noneutral ends@)esign.

That was the starting point of their 2 (@draw book@)008 book, Nudge.
    draw thaler

    erase book

B: Their proposal: if someone must design the menu anyway, design it to help people, as  (@draw judged ends@)judged by themselves.

    erase thaler

A nudge, in their sense, changes the setting: i (@draw t1@)t forbids nothing, leave (@draw t2@)s prices alone, and is easy a (@draw t3@)nd cheap to avoid.

B: They called i (@draw lp@)t libertarian paternalism. Isn't that a contradiction?

Their answer: paternalism, because someone steers you for your own good; libertarian, becaus (@highlight apple@)e you can still walk straight past the fruit  (@highlight cake effect circle@)to the cake.
    blocking false

A pension default you can leave with one form is the same idea.
    highlight t3 effect underline

    hide counter apple chips cake noneutral judged t1 t2 t3 lp

    draw sortq

    ask "Sort these six policies: nudge, or not a nudge?" intro "Now test the definition." on sortq store srt stage own voice b
        right: The tax and the ban are not nudges; the other four change only the setting.
        wrong: {srt} of {srt.total} first time: only the tax and the ban aren't nudges.

    show counter apple chips cake noneutral t1 t2 t3

Only the tax and the ban  (@draw fail@)fail: one changes the price, the other removes the option.

    erase fail

B: So the question isn't whether to steer. Somebody always does. It's who  (@draw who@)steers, toward what, and whether you can see it.

## How big is a nudge?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Journal papers
      - Nudge-unit trials
    values:
      - 8.7
      - 1.4
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: ' pp'
      decimals: 1
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_label: Rise in take-up, points
    colors:
      - grey
      - orange
    gap: 0.6
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 130
      w: 470
      h: 440
sources:
    - id: dl2022
      title: 'RCTs to Scale: Comprehensive Evidence From Two Nudge Units'
      authors: Stefano DellaVigna, Elizabeth Linos
      year: 2022
      finding: '126 RCTs, 23 million people, two US nudge units (OES, BIT North America): average effect 1.4 percentage points (about 8% over control); academic-journal nudges 8.7 pp (33.4%). Selective publication, with low power, explains about 70% of the gap.'
      doi: 10.3982/ECTA18709
    - id: mertens2022
      title: 'The effectiveness of nudging: A meta-analysis of choice architecture interventions across behavioral domains'
      authors: Stephanie Mertens, Mario Herberz, Ulf J. J. Hahnel, Tobias Brosch
      year: 2022
      finding: 'Over 200 studies, about 450 effect sizes: pooled effect d = 0.43 (small to medium).'
    - id: maier2022
      title: No evidence for nudging after adjusting for publication bias
      authors: Maximilian Maier, František Bartoš, T. D. Stanley, David R. Shanks, Adam J. L. Harris, Eric-Jan Wagenmakers
      year: 2022
      finding: Bias-corrected overall effect d ≈ 0.04 [0.00, 0.14]; severe publication bias; after correction no evidence remains for an average effect (Bayes factor undecided).
      doi: 10.1073/pnas.2200300119

    text uk "UK nudge unit, 2010" #8f887c x 790 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text trials "126 trials\n23 million people" #3d3833 cites ["dl2022"] x 790 y 560 font_size 32 hidden true
    text sixth "about ÷ 6" #b5482e cites ["dl2022"] x 790 y 470 font_size 44 hidden true
