    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch mth work ["No nudge: 17 in 100 act","1.4 ÷ 17 ≈ +8%","Journals: 8.7 ÷ 26 ≈ +33%"] x 810 y 320 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "How big is a nudge?"

B: Governments liked the idea, and built nudge units to r (@draw uk@)un it; Britain's came first, in 2010. So do nudges actually work?
    draw axes

B: Academic journals say yes: on average a nudge raised take-up, of a vaccine, a benefit, a pa (@draw bar_1@)yment on time, by about eight point seven percentage points.

That's a lot for a letter. I'd guess governments get about the same when they do it for real.
    highlight bar_1

    erase uk

    draw trials

    ask "Drag the nudge-unit bar to your guess, in points." on bar_2 store g default "5" voice b
        intro: Two American nudge units ran a hundred and twenty-six trials on twenty-three million people, and kept every result.
        right: Close: you said {g}; the nudge units found {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; the nudge units found {g.true}.

B: One point four points. Real, and reliable, but about a six (@draw sixth@)th of the journal number.

So where did the rest go?
    point at.ref bar_1 at.anchor top gesture circle

    erase trials

B: Mostly into the drawer. A small study tends to get publi (@draw drawer@)shed when it finds a big effect, and the flops stay unseen. DellaVigna and Linos put about seventy perc (@highlight drawer effect underline@)ent of the gap down to that.

The same fight broke out over a big 2022 meta-analysis by Mertens  (@draw mertens@)and colleagues: across hundreds of studies, a medium-sized effect.

B: Correct for the missing studies, Maier and colleagues rep (@draw maier@)lied, and no clear evidence of an average effect is left.

    erase mertens maier drawer

But hold one thing in view: cost. A letter costs pennies. Say (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@) a city sends a hundred thousand of them:

one point four points
    draw calc_line_2

is fourteen hundred more people who act.
    draw calc_line_3

    erase calc

B: So nudges are not magic, and not nothing: small, cheap, and very uneve (@draw stamp@)n from one to the next.

    draw mathq

    ask "Percent or percentage points? Open the math, or skip it." on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq stamp

B: Percentage points are not percent. In the nudge-unit trials, about seventeen in a hundred acted without the nudge.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

B: So one point four points is a rise of about eight percent.
    draw mth_line_2

B: In the journals the base was about twenty-six, so eight point seven points is a rise of about a third.
    draw mth_line_3

    erase mth

    show stamp

@after

    erase mathq

## Respect or manipulation?
sources:
    - id: loew2015
      title: 'Warning: You are about to be nudged'
      authors: George Loewenstein, Cindy Bryce, David Hagmann, Sachin Rajpal
      year: 2015
      finding: In hypothetical advance directives, telling people about the default before or after they chose did not significantly reduce the default's effect.
    - id: hausman2010
      title: 'Debate: To Nudge or Not to Nudge'
      authors: Daniel M. Hausman, Brynn Welch
      year: 2010
      finding: Nudges that work by exploiting flaws in deliberation rather than by persuasion raise concerns about autonomy and manipulation.
    - id: banuri2019
      title: Biased Policy Professionals
      authors: Sheheryar Banuri, Stefan Dercon, Varun Gauri
      year: 2019
      finding: World Bank and UK DFID staff showed framing effects and confirmation bias driven by ideological predisposition when interpreting data.
    - id: glaeser2006
      title: Paternalism and Psychology
      authors: Edward L. Glaeser
      year: 2006
      finding: Bounded rationality raises the costs of government decisions too; hard paternalism (taxes, bans) is easier to monitor and control than soft paternalism.
    - id: thaler2008b
      title: 'Nudge: Improving Decisions about Health, Wealth, and Happiness'
      authors: Richard H. Thaler, Cass R. Sunstein
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Endorses Rawls''s publicity principle: government should not adopt a policy it would not be able or willing to defend publicly.'
    - id: dl2022b
      title: 'RCTs to Scale: Comprehensive Evidence From Two Nudge Units'
      authors: Stefano DellaVigna, Elizabeth Linos
      year: 2022
      finding: Average nudge-unit effect 1.4 percentage points, against 8.7 in academic journals.

    box tagA "A: respectful steering" #2f6b8f x 255 y 605 width 440 height 76 radius 12 font_size 34 style.stroke #2f6b8f hidden true
    box tagB "B: risk of manipulation" #b5482e x 745 y 605 width 440 height 76 radius 12 font_size 34 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text a1 "Design is unavoidable" #3d3833 x 255 y 525 font_size 42 hidden true
    text a2 "Every option stays" #3d3833 x 255 y 465 font_size 42 hidden true
    text b1 "Works in the dark" #3d3833 x 745 y 525 font_size 42 hidden true
    mark strike1 around b1 kind strike hidden true
    text b1n "told the default: still works" #8f887c cites ["loew2015"] x 745 y 482 font_size 30 hidden true
    text b2 "✓ Nudgers are biased too" #4f8a3a cites ["banuri2019"] x 745 y 420 font_size 42 hidden true
    text b3 "Knowledge + incentives" #3d3833 x 745 y 360 font_size 42 hidden true
    text a3 "Granted: harder to watch" #8f887c cites ["glaeser2006"] x 255 y 420 font_size 38 hidden true
    text a4 "Publicity test" #2f6b8f cites ["thaler2008b"] x 255 y 360 font_size 42 hidden true

    card title "Respect or manipulation?"

So nudges are small and cheap. Should a government use them on (@draw tagA@) us? I'll argue yes.

B: And I'll argue we s (@draw tagB@)hould be wary: a nudge can be manipulation.

My case: someone designs the (@draw a1@) counter anyway. Better on purpose and in the open, aimed at what people themselves want, wi (@draw a2@)th every option kept.

B: My case: a nudge often works because (@draw b1@) we don't notice it. It goes around your reasoning instead of through it (@highlight b1 effect underline@); philosophers like Daniel Hausman and Brynn Welch pressed exactly that.

That part we can test. In a 2015 experiment, people were told up front which answer was ticked by default, and the defau (@draw strike1 ends@)lt still worked about a (@draw b1n@)s well.

B: Fair. Then who nudges the nudgers? Officials have bi (@draw b2@)ases too: in one experiment, World Bank and British aid staff read the same data differently depending on their politics.

B: And the state fails through what it can't kno (@draw b3@)w and what it's paid to want. A nudge escapes neither.

Granted. Edward Glaeser's point stings (@draw a3@): a tax sits in the budget for everyone to fight over, a quiet nudge is harder to watch.

That's why Thaler and Sunstein say: never nudge in a (@draw a4@) way you couldn't defend in public.

B: How sure are we? That nudge (@draw s1@)s work a little, at scale: mostly agreed.

Whether steering people by their own lights respects them or manipulates them: contes (@draw s2 ends@)ted. The data can't settle that one.

    erase s1 s2

    quiz "A town wants fewer cars in its centre. Which of these is a nudge?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: The trip-time sign: it changes what drivers see, forbids nothing and costs nothing. The charge changes the price, the ban removes the option.
        wrong: Apply the test: nothing forbidden, no price changed: here, the trip-time sign.
        * A congestion charge
        + A sign at the car park comparing trip times by bus and by car
        * A weekday ban on cars

    quiz "A calorie label, tried in one canteen, cut the calories people ordered by 10 percent. The government rolls it out in every canteen in the country. What should you expect?"
        right: Something smaller: striking pilots are the ones that get noticed, and at scale the average nudge did far less.
        wrong: Think about which small trials get noticed and published: here, something smaller.
        * About 10 percent
        + Something smaller
        * More, because more people see it

    quiz "A council makes one energy company the default supplier for new residents. Switching takes a minute online, and the company's boss chairs the council's business forum. Which objection fits best?" voice b
        right: Who nudges the nudgers. It is a real nudge, easy to leave, but the person who picked the default stands to gain from it.
        wrong: Nothing is forbidden and no price changed, so look at who chose the default: here, who nudges the nudgers.
        + Who nudges the nudgers: whose interest set the default?
        * It forbids other suppliers
        * It raises the price of energy

    text s1 "Nudges work a little, at scale: mostly agreed" #3d3833 x 500 y 285 font_size 36
    text s2 "Respect or manipulation: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 228 font_size 40

B: Every counter is laid out by someone. The real question isn't nudge or no nudge, but who holds the pen, and whether we can watch them write.
    highlight tagA tagB

